    "q-163211": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163211",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=xy.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $\\frac{2}{3}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}(2-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{10}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-8+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{8}{15})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$",
