    "q-163209": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163209",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-y\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $\\frac{1}{15}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{x=-y}^{x=0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot (0-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{y}^{6}}{24}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{24})}{(\\frac{1}{15})}=-\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
